            <ArticleTitle>[Bilateral subdural hematoma secondary to accidental dural puncture].</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>306-9</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.bjan.2014.07.002</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0034-7094(14)00200-1</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>We report the case of a 25-year-old woman, who received epidural analgesia for labour pain and subsequently presented post-dural puncture headache. Conservative treatment was applied and epidural blood patch was performed. In the absence of clinical improvement and due to changes in the postural component of the headache, a brain imaging test was performed showing a bilateral subdural hematoma. The post-dural puncture headache is relatively common, but the lack of response to established medical treatment as well as the change in its characteristics and the presence of neurological deficit, should raise the suspicion of a subdural hematoma, which although is rare, can be lethal if not diagnosed and treated at the right time.</AbstractText>
